{
  "requirement": "The review prompt's criteria MUST direct the reviewer to find each of the following:\n\n- text that a careful reviewer could read two ways;\n- obligations that a reviewer could not judge from the requirement text, the code, and the tests, or that nothing would fail for if they were broken;\n- text that prescribes an implementation instead of an outcome. Public API\n  declarations specify an observable interface and are appropriate contract\n  text; their accompanying comments can specify semantic guarantees.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The requirement obliges the review prompt's criteria to direct the reviewer to find three kinds of text. The first is text readable two ways. The second is obligations that can't be judged, or that nothing would fail for if broken. The third is text that prescribes an implementation instead of an outcome. The third kind carries a note that public API declarations and their comments are appropriate contract text.\n\n`tests/authoring_cli.rs#fn[the_review_prompt_criteria_find_ambiguous_uncheckable_and_prescriptive_text]` carries the `{test}` reference. It takes the `## Criteria` section of the prompt through `review_prompt()` and `section()`, then calls `assert_text`. `assert_text` asserts `contains` for each phrase and fails with a labelled message if one is missing.\n\n| Obligation | Phrase asserted |\n|---|---|\n| Text read two ways | `**One reading:** text a careful reviewer could read two ways is a finding` |\n| Uncheckable obligations, or ones nothing would fail for | `**Checkable:** a reviewer can judge the obligation from the requirement text, the code, and the tests, and something would fail if it were broken` |\n| Prescribing implementation instead of outcome | `**Outcome, not implementation:** the text says what holds` |\n| Public API carve-out | \"Public API declarations specify an observable interface\", \"their accompanying comments can specify semantic guarantees\", and \"Distinguish these from prescribing internal implementation details that the contract does not need\" |\n\nI read `assets/skills/habeas-requirements/references/review-prompt.md`. Its `## Criteria` section contains these phrases. Removing or rewording a criterion or the carve-out would fail the test. The test is scoped to the Criteria section, which matches the requirement. The requirement constrains prompt text, so a text-presence test is the right level. I did not read the body of `section()` past its truncated excerpt, so I relied on the evidence rationale's statement that it collapses whitespace. The asserted phrases match the prompt, so this does not change the result. I found no gaps, no ambiguity and no unmarked relevant tests.",
